    "cerebras_mesh_source": "configs/hardware/technology.json#links.on_wafer_n5.hop_latency_s",
    "express_link_field_crossing_ns": 30.0,
    "speedup_over_cerebras_mesh": 4.17
  },
  "conclusion": "On ASAP7 a flow-repeated wire costs 605 ps/mm register to register, 4.0x the 150 ps/mm technology.json assumes and well above its 100-250 range: ASAP7's routing metals are thin (M6/M8 at 64/80 nm pitch) and ORFS repeats with buffers it sizes for timing, not an optimally sized inverter chain, so this is a pessimistic 7 nm point and an N5 top-metal link would be faster. Even so a purpose-built express link crosses a 28.55 mm field in 30 cycles = 30 ns at 1 GHz with a forwarding boundary (35 ns with a one-shot FP32 reduction), about 4.2x less than the 125 ns Cerebras-mesh crossing the model charges. The latency a field crossing should carry for a purpose-built collective network is therefore ~30 ns (35 ns reducing), and a single unregistered reticle traversal at the measured slope is 17.3 ns, not the 4.28 ns the 150 ps/mm assumption gives",
